      "fact": "The Supreme Court decided United States v. X-Citement Video, Inc., 513 U.S. 64 in 1994.",
      "source_date": "1994-06-21"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "fact": "In United States v. X-Citement Video, Inc. (1994), the Supreme Court held that statutory interpretation of federal criminal laws must be precise.",
      "source_date": "1994-06-21"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "fact": "In United States v. X-Citement Video, Inc. (1994), the Supreme Court held that ambiguity in a statute's intent element must be resolved in favor of the defendant under the rule of lenity.",
      "source_date": "1994-06-21"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "fact": "The Supreme Court decided Schlup v. Delo, 513 U.S. 298 in 1995.",
      "source_date": "1995-05-18"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "fact": "In Schlup v. Delo (1995), the Supreme Court established that a federal prisoner may bypass the AEDPA's bar on second or successive habeas petitions under the 'savings clause' of 28 U.S.C. § 2255(e) only if they can make a threshold showing of actual innocence.",
